{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788286211,"nonce":"i53PzL4p6Ij5h0Eabb1j4oTdpmk=","title":"place-layout reports a shortfall that does not exist: short_by is a conclusion drawn from a stockpile-only count","message":"Found by the M2 rehearsal (session 18), on the run that met M2 — so this is a\nmeasurement, not a review note.\n\n## What happened\n\n`place-layout templates/bedroom.ir.json --origin 119,126 --stuff '*=WoodLog'`\non a fresh quicktest map with 869 unforbidden `WoodLog` lying ten cells from the\nsite and NO stockpile zone anywhere on the map:\n\n    materials  [{\"def\":\"WoodLog\",\"count\":185,\"in_stockpiles\":0}]\n    shortfall  [{\"def\":\"WoodLog\",\"needed\":185,\"in_stockpiles\":0,\"short_by\":185}]\n\nDanielle then hauled and built all 22 elements between tick 1264 and tick 4444\nwithout a single stall. Nothing was short. `construction`'s `missing[]` said the\nsame thing by the same route and was wrong for the same reason.\n\n## Why `in_stockpiles` is fine and `short_by` is not\n\n`LayoutVerbs.MaterialBill`'s own header already states the true fact:\n\n\u003e `in_stockpiles` is `map.resourceCounter`, which walks SlotGroup haul\n\u003e destinations, so goods lying on unzoned ground read as ZERO. [...] \"we have no\n\u003e steel\" and \"the steel is not in a stockpile\" are different problems and this\n\u003e number cannot tell them apart.\n\nThat comment is correct, and it indicts the code three lines below it.\n`in_stockpiles` is a MEASUREMENT whose name says exactly what it measured — keep\nit. `short_by`, and the existence of a row in `shortfall[]` at all, is a\nCONCLUSION, and it is derived (`stored \u003c kv.Value`) from the number the comment\njust said cannot support it. `ConstructionVerbs.Stored` feeds `missing[]` the\nsame way.\n\nThis is the project's own \"candidates + reasons, never bare booleans\" rule at one\nremove: the agent is handed a verdict manufactured from a partial count, with the\ndisclaimer sitting in a source comment it cannot read.\n\nIt is not a corner case. It is the DEFAULT state of every fresh map: quicktest\ngenerates no stockpile, and `dev:starter-kit` did not create one either despite\n`stockpile:true` (`zones` reported `stockpiles.total: 0` after two kit calls —\nworth a look on its own, but the shortfall bug outlives any fix to that).\n\n## What the game's own availability predicate is\n\nVerified in the decompiled 1.6 tree, by member:\n\n- `RimWorld/WorkGiver_ConstructDeliverResources.ResourceDeliverJobFor` reaches\n  material through\n  `GenClosest.ClosestThingReachable(pawn.Position, pawn.Map,\n  ThingRequest.ForDef(need.thingDef), PathEndMode.ClosestTouch,\n  TraverseParms.For(pawn), 9999f, (Thing r) =\u003e ResourceValidator(pawn, need, r))`.\n- `ResourceValidator` (`:34`) is three clauses: `th.def != need.thingDef`,\n  `th.IsForbidden(pawn)`, `!HaulAIUtility.PawnCanAutomaticallyHaulFast(pawn, th,\n  forced: false)`.\n- `FindAvailableNearbyResources` (`:182`) then widens by\n  `GenRadial.RadialDistinctThingsAround(..., 5f, useCenter: false)`.\n- **`SlotGroup`, `slotGroup`, `haulDestination`, `Stockpile` and\n  `resourceCounter` do not appear anywhere in that file.** Grepped over the\n  whole of it, unpiped.\n\nSo the game asks \"is there a reachable, unforbidden stack of this def\" and never\nasks whether it is zoned. The mod is answering a question the builder does not\nask, and then drawing a builder's conclusion from the answer.\n\n## Scope\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/LayoutVerbs.cs` `MaterialBill` and\n`Source/AutoRimmer/ConstructionVerbs.cs` `Stored` / `missing[]`. `DigestVerb`'s\n`resources` section has the same stockpile scope but already publishes\n`scope: \"stockpiles-only\"` beside it and draws no conclusion, so it is honest as\nit stands — though it should be checked against whatever wording lands here.\n\nThe fix is NOT to delete the shortfall. An agent genuinely needs to know it\ncannot build. The fix is to make the conclusion come from the predicate the\nbuilder uses: count reachable-and-unforbidden stacks of the def, keep\n`in_stockpiles` beside it as the separate fact it is, and let `short_by` mean\nwhat its name says.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- On a map with the material loose on unzoned ground and reachable, a\n  `place-layout` dry run returns an EMPTY `shortfall[]`, and the bill carries\n  both counts distinguishably (the zoned figure and the available figure).\n- On a map with the material present but FORBIDDEN, `shortfall[]` is non-empty\n  and says so — forbidden and absent are different, and `unforbid` is the fix for\n  one and not the other. Same for material that exists but is unreachable.\n- With genuinely no material on the map, `shortfall[]` is non-empty and\n  `short_by` equals the full bill.\n- `construction`'s `missing[]` answers consistently with `place-layout`'s\n  `shortfall[]` on all three, since both claim to describe the same thing.\n- The availability count cites its vanilla provenance in a comment the way\n  `Blockers.cs` does — file + member — and does not re-enter a lazy getter per\n  element (observers never mutate; a per-element map scan is a cost note too).\n- Re-run of the M2 bedroom placement: bill 185 WoodLog, `shortfall[]` empty,\n  room still built. That is the exact case that produced this issue.","files":null}]}